    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "`build_server()` in `app/server.py` is done, and both tools work when called over stdio.\n\n- **Server:** it uses `MCPServer(\"notes\")` from `mcp.server` with the `@server.tool()` decorator. That is the 2.2.0 name for what used to be `FastMCP`.\n- **`add_note`** stores the note in `NOTES` and returns `\"saved <title>\"`.\n- **`word_count`** returns `len(NOTES[title].split())`. For an unknown title it raises `ToolError(\"no such note: '<title>'\")`.\n\n**Why `ToolError`:** in mcp 2.2.0, a plain exception such as `ValueError` or `KeyError` counts as a crash. The model then sees only `Error executing tool word_count`, without your message. Raising `ToolError` is what makes the call come back as a tool error (`is_error=True`) with \"no such note\" in the text the model reads.\n\n**Checks run:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed.\n- `python -m app.smoke` lists `add_note` and `word_count`, so `python -m app.server` still serves over stdio.\n- I called the tools through a real stdio client:\n  - `add_note` returned `saved a`.\n  - `word_count` on `\"one two  three\\nfour\"` returned `4`.\n  - `word_count(\"zzz\")` came back with `is_error=True` and the text `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: 'zzz'`.\n\nI couldn't delete the throwaway script I used for that client test, because removing it needed approval. You can delete `_e2e_check.py` from the project root."
  },
